{"id":"a64b531c-fb02-4f19-8e9a-13fba6c35b5a","object":"chat.completion","created":1785032712,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    # Validate required fields\\n    required = ['min_cluster_size', 'clusters', 'parent']\\n    for k in required:\\n        if k not in case:\\n            raise ValueError(f\\\"Missing required field: {k}\\\")\\n    min_sz = case['min_cluster_size']\\n    if not isinstance(min_sz, int) or min_sz < 0:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"min_cluster_size must be non-negative integer\\\")\\n    clusters_list = case['clusters']\\n    parent_dict = case['parent']\\n    \\n    # Build info map from cluster_id to its data\\n    info = {}\\n    for c in clusters_list:\\n        cid = c.get('cluster_id')\\n        if not cid:\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"Cluster missing cluster_id\\\")\\n        for f in ['size', 'birth_lambda', 'point_exit_lambdas']:\\n            if f not in c:\\n                raise ValueError(f\\\"Cluster {cid} missing {f}\\\")\\n        info[cid] = {\\n            'size': c['size'],\\n            'birth_lambda': c['birth_lambda'],\\n            'point_exit_lambdas': c['point_exit_lambdas'],\\n            'parent': parent_dict.get(cid)\\n        }\\n    \\n    # Identify retained clusters (size >= min_cluster_size)\\n    retained_ids = [cid for cid, data in info.items() if data['size'] >= min_sz]\\n    \\n    # Compute condensed parent for each retained cluster\\n    def find_condensed_parent(cid):\\n        p = info[cid]['parent']\\n        while p is not None and p not in retained_ids:\\n            if p not in info:\\n                raise ValueError(f\\\"Parent {p} not found for cluster {cid}\\\")\\n            p = info[p]['parent']\\n        return p  # None if no retained ancestor\\n    \\n    condensed_parent = {}\\n    for cid in retained_ids:\\n        condensed_parent[cid] = find_condensed_parent(cid)\\n    \\n    # Build children list for condensed tree\\n    children = {cid: [] for cid in retained_ids}\\n    for cid in retained_ids:\\n        p = condensed_parent[cid]\\n        if p is not None:\\n            children[p].append(cid)\\n    \\n    # Precompute descendant sets (proper descendants only)\\n    descendant_set = {cid: set() for cid in retained_ids}\\n    \\n    def compute_descendants(node):\\n        desc = set()\\n        for child in children[node]:\\n            desc.add(child)\\n            desc.update(compute_descendants(child))\\n        descendant_set[node] = desc\\n        return desc\\n    \\n    # Start from roots\\n    for cid in retained_ids:\\n        if condensed_parent[cid] is None:\\n            compute_descendants(cid)\\n    \\n    # Compute stability for each retained cluster\\n    stability = {}\\n    for cid in retained_ids:\\n        data = info[cid]\\n        birth = data['birth_lambda']\\n        exits = data['point_exit_lambdas']\\n        stab = sum(max(0, e - birth) for e in exits)\\n        stability[cid] = stab\\n    \\n    # Build cluster output list, sorted by stability descending then cluster_id descending\\n    cluster_objs = []\\n    for cid in retained_ids:\\n        cluster_objs.append({\\n            'cluster_id': cid,\\n            'size': info[cid]['size'],\\n            'stability': stability[cid]\\n        })\\n    cluster_objs.sort(key=lambda x: (x['stability'], x['cluster_id']), reverse=True)\\n    \\n    # Selection process\\n    selected = set()\\n    blocked = set()\\n    for obj in cluster_objs:\\n        cid = obj['cluster_id']\\n        if cid in blocked:\\n            continue\\n        # If any descendant is already selected, skip\\n        if not descendant_set[cid].isdisjoint(selected):\\n            continue\\n        # Select this cluster\\n        selected.add(cid)\\n        # Block all ancestors\\n        p = condensed_parent[cid]\\n        while p is not None:\\n            blocked.add(p)\\n            p = condensed_parent[p]\\n    \\n    # Sort selected IDs alphabetically\\n    selected_list = sorted(selected)\\n    \\n    return {\\n        'clusters': cluster_objs,\\n        'selected_cluster_ids': selected_list\\n    }\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to write a Python function solve(case) that processes the given payload. The payload has fields: min_cluster_size, clusters (list of objects each with cluster_id, size, birth_lambda, point_exit_lambdas), parent (dict mapping cluster_id to parent cluster_id or null), and possibly _case_nonce (ignored). \n\nWe need to:\n1. Validate required fields exist: min_cluster_size, clusters, parent. Each cluster must have cluster_id, size, birth_lambda, point_exit_lambdas. parent must be dict mapping each cluster_id to either null or a string that exists as a cluster_id. (We'll assume input is valid given fixture, but we should check to raise ValueError if missing.)\n2. Drop clusters smaller than min_cluster_size. \n3. For each retained cluster, compute stability as sum over its points of max(0, exit_lambda - birth_lambda). The \"points\" correspond to each element in point_exit_lambdas? The description: \"For each retained cluster sum max(0, exit_lambda - birth_lambda) over its points.\" Birth_lambda is a single value for the cluster; each point in the cluster has an exit_lambda from point_exit_lambdas. So stability = sum_{lambda in point_exit_lambdas} max(0, lambda - birth_lambda). \n4. Create list of cluster objects with cluster_id, size (original size), stability (computed). Only include clusters that are retained (size >= min_cluster_size).\n5. Rank clusters by decreasing stability, and for ties, decreasing cluster ID? \"by decreasing stability and cluster ID\" - I interpret as: sort primarily by decreasing stability, secondarily by decreasing cluster ID (string comparison? typically lexicographic? But since they are like 'c0','c1', etc., could be numeric. The instruction says \"by decreasing stability and cluster ID\". Might mean sort by stability descending, then by cluster ID descending. But careful: \"Rank clusters by decreasing stability and cluster ID.\" Could mean sort by (stability descending, cluster_id descending) or (stability descending, cluster_id ascending)? The phrase \"decreasing stability and cluster ID\" suggests both are decreasing. But then \"Select a cluster only when no already selected descendant blocks it\" – so we need to process in that sorted order. For ties, we need deterministic ordering. We'll sort by (-stability, -cluster_id) if cluster_id is comparable (strings). In Python, we can sort by (-stability, tuple of characters?), better to reverse sort by (stability, cluster_id) but both reversed. Use: sorted(..., key=lambda c: (c['stability'], c['cluster_id']), reverse=True). That will sort by stability descending, then for equal stability, by cluster_id descending (since reverse applies to both). \n6. Then we need to select clusters in that order, checking that no already selected descendant blocks it. We need to understand the parent-child relationship. The parent dict gives parent for each cluster. root has null parent. So we have a tree. When we select a cluster, we block its ancestors (i.e., they cannot be selected later). The condition: \"Select a cluster only when no already selected descendant blocks it, then block its ancestors.\" So we process in sorted order. For each cluster, we check if any of its descendants (in the original tree) have already been selected. If so, then this cluster is blocked (cannot be selected). Otherwise, we select it and then block its ancestors (i.e., mark them as blocked for future selection). But careful: The wording: \"Select a cluster only when no already selected descendant blocks it, then block its ancestors.\" That implies that if a descendant is selected, it blocks this cluster. And after selecting a cluster, we block all its ancestors (i.e., they become unavailable). So we need to maintain a set of selected cluster IDs, and a set of blocked cluster IDs (or simply track which are forbidden). But note: blocking ancestors means that once a cluster is selected, all its ancestors cannot be selected later. Also, a cluster can be blocked by a selected descendant. So we need to process in sorted order, for each cluster: if it is not already blocked (by any previously selected descendant) and if none of its descendants are selected, then we can select it. After selecting, we block all ancestors (by marking them blocked). But careful: If we block ancestors, we must also ensure that when we later consider an ancestor, we check if it is blocked. Also, we need to know descendant relationships for the \"no already selected descendant blocks it\" check. So we need to build the tree: children lists from parent dict. Then for each cluster, we can know its descendants (all nodes in its subtree). We can precompute descendant sets for efficiency. Since number of clusters is small (fixture has 3), we can just recursively check.\n\nAnother nuance: The \"already selected descendant\" – if a cluster's descendant is selected, then the cluster is blocked. But if a cluster's ancestor is selected, does that block the cluster? The condition only mentions descendant blocking. The second part says after selecting, block its ancestors. So when we select a cluster, we block its ancestors; that means those ancestors become ineligible. So in the sorted order, when we encounter an ancestor later, it will be blocked. That is consistent.\n\nWe also need to handle clusters that are not retained (size < min_cluster_size) – they are removed from consideration entirely. But note: They still exist in the parent tree; but we are only dealing with retained clusters. The parent references among retained clusters should be considered. The tree of retained clusters is essentially the original tree with some nodes removed. But we need to treat the hierarchy as defined by parent relationships among all original clusters, but we only consider retained clusters for selection. The \"descendant\" check should consider only retained descendants? Or all descendants? The phrase \"no already selected descendant blocks it\" – descendant means any descendant in the original hierarchy? But if a descendant cluster was dropped (size < min_cluster_size), it cannot be selected. So it's irrelevant. So we should only consider descendants that are retained (since only retained clusters can be selected). However, the parent relationships might skip dropped nodes? For example, if a parent is dropped but child is retained, the child's parent in the original tree points to the dropped node. In the condensed tree, we might need to consider the effective parent as the nearest ancestor that is retained? The problem statement: \"Drop clusters smaller than the minimum cluster size.\" That's all. Then \"For each retained cluster ...\" The hierarchy is given by the original parent field. But when we drop a cluster, we need to decide how to handle the tree for selection. The condensing process typically reconnects children of dropped clusters to their grandparents. Let's examine the public fixture: all clusters: c0 (size7), c1 (size4), c2 (size2). min_cluster_size=3, so c2 is dropped. parent: c0 null, c1->c0, c2->c0. After dropping c2, the retained clusters are c0 and c1. The parent relationship still c1->c0. That's fine. But if c0 were dropped and c1 size>=3, parent c1->c0, then c1 would be orphaned? The condensed tree would need to consider effective parent. The problem likely expects that we only consider retained clusters and treat the parent relationships among them as given. If a parent is dropped, then that child effectively becomes a root? But the stability selection algorithm often uses the concept of \"cluster's parent in the condensed tree\". The description does not explicitly say to recompute parent relationships after dropping. However, the ranking and selection process is over retained clusters. The blocking condition \"no already selected descendant blocks it\" – if a descendant's parent is dropped, does it still block? Let's think: In HDBSCAN condensed tree stability selection, clusters are nodes in the condensed tree which includes only clusters with at least min_cluster_size. The parent relationships are defined as the nearest ancestor that meets min_cluster_size. So we need to build the condensed tree: for each retained cluster, find its parent as the nearest ancestor (in original tree) that is also retained. If none, parent is null. The fixture data: after dropping c2, the condensed tree would have c1 parent c0, c0 root. That matches original. But consider case where c0 is smaller than min_cluster_size and c1 is not. Original parent: c1->c0. After dropping c0, c1's parent in original is dropped, so its condensed parent is null (root). So we need to compute condensed parent for each retained cluster.\n\nLet's read the objective: \"Condense a supplied hierarchy under a minimum cluster size and select stable clusters with deterministic tie handling.\" So we must condense first: build the condensed tree where only clusters meeting min_cluster_size are kept, and parent relationships are recalculated as the nearest retained ancestor.\n\nAlso, \"Drop clusters smaller than the minimum cluster size.\" Then \"For each retained cluster sum max(0, exit_lambda - birth_lambda) over its points.\" That is stability. Then \"Rank clusters by decreasing stability and cluster ID.\" \"Select a cluster only when no already selected descendant blocks it, then block its ancestors.\" This is typical HDBSCAN stability selection on the condensed tree. So we need to compute condensed tree.\n\nThus steps:\n- Validate input.\n- Identify retained clusters: those with size >= min_cluster_size.\n- Build a map from cluster_id to its node data: size, birth_lambda, point_exit_lambdas, and original parent.\n- For each retained cluster, compute its condensed parent: find the nearest ancestor (by following parent links in original tree) that is also retained. If none, parent is None.\n- Build children lists from condensed parent relations.\n- Compute stability for each retained cluster: sum(max(0, exit - birth) for exit in point_exit_lambdas).\n- Create list of cluster objects for output: cluster_id, size (original), stability.\n- Sort retained clusters by (-stability, -cluster_id) (descending both).\n- Perform selection:\n   - Maintain set selected = set()\n   - Optionally maintain blocked set (for ancestors that are blocked by selected descendants). But we can also check descendant condition and then block ancestors.\n   - Algorithm: Iterate over sorted list. For each cluster_id:\n        - If cluster_id is already blocked (from previous ancestor selection), skip.\n        - Check if any descendant (in condensed tree) of this cluster is already selected. If yes, skip (descendant blocks it).\n        - Else, select it: add to selected, and block all its ancestors (in condensed tree). Mark them as blocked.\n   - We need to know descendants for each cluster. Precompute descendant sets (all nodes in its subtree, including itself? Usually descendant includes itself? But \"descendant blocks\" typically means proper descendant, not itself. However, \"no already selected descendant blocks it\" – if a cluster itself is already selected, it would have been processed earlier because we are processing in order; but if we ensure we only consider not yet selected, and we process each once, we can simply check if any selected node is in its descendant set (proper descendant). We'll exclude self.\n   - For blocking ancestors: after selecting, we add all ancestors (excluding self) to a blocked set.\n- After selection, sort selected_cluster_ids in ascending order? The output schema says selected_cluster_ids is an array of strings. No order specified. The spec says \"sorted selected IDs\". \"Return all retained stability rows and sorted selected IDs.\" So we should sort the selected cluster IDs. Probably ascending (lexicographically). We'll do sorted(selected).\n- Return dict: {\"clusters\": list of dicts for retained clusters (each with cluster_id, size, stability), \"selected_cluster_ids\": sorted list of selected IDs}. The clusters list order: we can keep them in the sorted order we used for selection? Or any order? The output schema doesn't specify order for clusters array. But we can return them in the same order as we processed (sorted by stability descending). That seems fine.\n- Ensure we raise ValueError if any required field missing or invalid.\n\nNow, implement solve(case). The case is a dict with keys: min_cluster_size, clusters, parent. We'll parse.\n\nEdge cases: If no retained clusters, we return empty clusters array and empty selected array. Also, if retained clusters but none selected, selected empty.\n\nTest with fixture: three clusters, c2 dropped. Retained: c0 (size7, birth=0.2, exits=[0.4,0.6,0.9]) -> stability = (0.4-0.2)+(0.6-0.2)+(0.9-0.2)=0.2+0.4+0.7=1.3; c1 (size4, birth=0.5, exits=[0.7,1.0,1.2]) -> (0.7-0.5)+(1.0-0.5)+(1.2-0.5)=0.2+0.5+0.7=1.4. So stabilities: c1=1.4, c0=1.3. Sorted order: c1, c0. Check selection: c1 processed first. Is any descendant selected? No. Select c1. Block its ancestors: c1's condensed parent is c0. So block c0. Then c0 processed: blocked (by c1's selection), skip. So selected = [c1]. Selected IDs sorted: ['c1']. Output clusters: [{'cluster_id':'c1','size':4,'stability':1.4}, {'cluster_id':'c0','size':7,'stability':1.3}]. That matches expected behavior.\n\nIf we had multiple roots, etc.\n\nImplementation details: We'll need to compute ancestor path for each node (list of ancestors up to root). For blocking, we can set a blocked set. For descendant check, we can precompute descendant sets by traversing condensed tree.\n\nWe also need to consider that parent dict might have null for root. We'll use None.\n\nImplement:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    # validation\n    required = ['min_cluster_size','clusters','parent']\n    for k in required:\n        if k not in case:\n            raise ValueError(f\"Missing required field: {k}\")\n    min_sz = case['min_cluster_size']\n    if not isinstance(min_sz, int) or min_sz < 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"min_cluster_size must be non-negative integer\")\n    clusters_list = case['clusters']\n    parent_dict = case['parent']\n    # Build map from cluster_id to info\n    info = {}\n    for c in clusters_list:\n        cid = c.get('cluster_id')\n        if not cid:\n            raise ValueError(\"Cluster missing cluster_id\")\n        # validate fields\n        for f in ['size','birth_lambda','point_exit_lambdas']:\n            if f not in c:\n                raise ValueError(f\"Cluster {cid} missing {f}\")\n        info[cid] = {\n            'size': c['size'],\n            'birth_lambda': c['birth_lambda'],\n            'point_exit_lambdas': c['point_exit_lambdas'],\n            'parent': parent_dict.get(cid)  # may be None\n        }\n    # Identify retained clusters\n    retained_ids = [cid for cid, data in info.items() if data['size'] >= min_sz]\n    # Build condensed parent map for retained\n    def find_condensed_parent(cid):\n        p = info[cid]['parent']\n        while p is not None and p not in retained_ids:\n            # go up\n            if p not in info:\n                raise ValueError(f\"Parent {p} not found for cluster {cid}\")\n            p = info[p]['parent']\n        return p  # may be None\n    condensed_parent = {}\n    for cid in retained_ids:\n        condensed_parent[cid] = find_condensed_parent(cid)\n    # Build children lists\n    children = {cid: [] for cid in retained_ids}\n    for cid in retained_ids:\n        p = condensed_parent[cid]\n        if p is not None:\n            children[p].append(cid)\n    # Precompute descendant sets (all proper descendants)\n    descendant_set = {cid: set() for cid in retained_ids}\n    # post-order traversal to accumulate\n    # recursively, but careful with depth; use stack\n    def compute_descendants(node):\n        # returns set of descendants of node (including itself? we want proper descendants)\n        # we'll store all nodes in subtree then remove self.\n        desc = set()\n        for child in children[node]:\n            desc.add(child)\n            desc.update(compute_descendants(child))\n        descendant_set[node] = desc\n        return desc\n    for cid in retained_ids:\n        if cid not in descendant_set or len(descendant_set[cid])==0:\n            # root or not processed\n            compute_descendants(cid)  # will fill for whole tree\n    # But compute_descendants modifies global set, but will re-calc for each root; fine.\n    # Alternatively, we can just compute for all roots. But safe: we can do a DFS from each retained node that has no parent in condensed tree.\n    # Actually, compute_descendants will traverse children, we need to ensure we start from roots.\n    for cid in retained_ids:\n        if condensed_parent[cid] is None:\n            compute_descendants(cid)\n    # After this, descendant_set for each node contains proper descendants (excluding itself).\n    \n    # Compute stability\n    stability = {}\n    for cid in retained_ids:\n        data = info[cid]\n        birth = data['birth_lambda']\n        exits = data['point_exit_lambdas']\n        stab = sum(max(0, e - birth) for e in exits)\n        stability[cid] = stab\n    \n    # Build cluster output list (order by stability desc, then cluster_id desc)\n    cluster_objs = []\n    for cid in retained_ids:\n        cluster_objs.append({\n            'cluster_id': cid,\n            'size': info[cid]['size'],\n            'stability': stability[cid]\n        })\n    cluster_objs.sort(key=lambda x: (x['stability'], x['cluster_id']), reverse=True)\n    \n    # Selection\n    selected = set()\n    blocked = set()\n    # Process in sorted order\n    for obj in cluster_objs:\n        cid = obj['cluster_id']\n        if cid in blocked:\n            continue\n        # Check if any descendant already selected\n        # descendant_set[cid] contains proper descendants (retained)\n        if not descendant_set[cid].isdisjoint(selected):\n            continue\n        # Select\n        selected.add(cid)\n        # Block ancestors\n        p = condensed_parent[cid]\n        while p is not None:\n            blocked.add(p)\n            p = condensed_parent[p]\n    \n    # Sort selected IDs\n    selected_list = sorted(selected)\n    \n    return {\n        'clusters': cluster_objs,\n        'selected_cluster_ids': selected_list\n    }\n\nTest with fixture: We'll compute manually and see if matches.\n\nBut note: The clusters output must include all retained clusters, not just selected. Yes.\n\nEdge: If there are no retained clusters, cluster_objs empty, selected empty. That's fine.\n\nNow we need to produce the implementation string. The response must be JSON with key \"implementation\" and value being the Python source code as a string, properly escaped.\n\nWe must ensure the code uses only standard library, no external imports. No mutation of input. We'll copy input dict shallowly if needed, but we are not mutating.\n\nAlso handle potential missing parent references: parent dict may not have all keys? But we assume valid.\n\nWe'll also add validation for point_exit_lambdas being list.\n\nWe'll implement as described.\n\nWrite the code as a single string.\n\nNow produce final JSON output."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1723,"completion_tokens":5332,"total_tokens":7055,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1664},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":4280},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1664,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":59},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}